  and that's going to be the temperature  which you know I'm not  for for some operational issues normally  hydrates but the nice thing is because  it's you know it's an interpolating  function I can also look at these  results not just in the time domain but  I can also look at I can look them out  in the spatial domain and sort of the  radial distance and the pipeline so how  do we decide to develop Alex we had a  couple options we had Excel Visual  Studio we had Mathematica and  you know I'm obviously a huge supporter  of Mathematica but at the end of the day  what it really came down to was that we  needed something that we could dealt  very fast like we're not we're not in  the R&amp;D business we're in the you know  clients and project business and  something so we needed needed something  that worked it was accurate and and we  could build it really quickly and so and  develop 37 different utilities inside of  Visual Basic is not something that's  gonna take the amount of time that we  needed it to or though it doesn't matter  of time that we had so so the code  structure is so that the rest of the  slides that I just want to go through  really kind of briefly on each one how  you know I've approached a certain issue  related to application development with  the Wolfram language and and just to  give you guys an idea of how it's all  set up so alex is contained in a package  that's really nice for naming  conventions it makes it really easy to  isolate your code and using the  different you know the colon key or  whatever you can isolate it and grab a  particular variable from a certain  context I also like the package editor  basically just because it allows me to  collapse these different sections and I  can organize it really nicely I can I  can have you know an image individual  module and then everything related to  that and and isolate out the methods  from the UI and things like that and the  other thing that's nice is when you have  it in a package as opposed to see yes  you can protect that and you can encode  it and and that way your intellectual  property safe so the inside sender the  UI really quick the whole so the whole  thing is obviously in the CDF on the top  we've got a docked cell and then the  remainder of the the notebook runs is  all wrapped around a dynamic module  wrapped inside of a dynamic module and  then each individual utility  has its own dynamic module and then  inside of that is a pain because a lot  of these extend beyond the on the screen  and we wanted something that we could  scroll through and then over here that  search will feel that I mentioned before  and then just a pain here with a with a  column of buttons in each one if you  click it you know it sets the the  appropriate utility that you want to  view so all of us have written  mathematical code we know that there's  no compiling and that's that's what's  great about it I did want to have  something that would allow me to build  Aleks and what it basically does is I  wrote this function that compiles to  compile Aleks and it and it takes the  file as a package and it moves it over  to the appropriate user base directory  for Mathematica and for C or for the CDF  player and in the process it encodes it  so that it's it's protected and you  can't can't view it in raw text form and  what it also will do is update a version  number that that I cannot maintain  separate from the utility and then if I  want I can it has an option that it will  accept its own version number so if I  want to increment the next version  number I can do that manually so version  control it's you know it's critical for  any successful software project and Alex  because it's in a package form and it's  a text form it's very easy to maintain  it using it and because it's raw text  you can do diffs on the different  versions really easily so it's it's nice  as opposed to using a notebook which has  a lot of cells and a different a lot of  additional text if you actually looked  inside of it and and that makes it a  little bit more difficult for the  version control updates when I first  arted working on Alex we was updates  sometimes twice a week and I always  wanted to make sure that people had an  easy way to get the latest version so I  have all the files sort of associated  with Alex in one central spot and then I  wrote this little executable here and  that'll copy those files to the the  appropriate spot on their computer  system under there under that same user  user base directory in that way when the  when the Alex launches it knows exactly  where to go to look for those for those  files and it can load them in really  easily  so this is something I I've only  recently started doing actually and I  knew that Wolfram had automated testing  but I hadn't had a chance to really look  at it yet and and once I did I was I was  really impressed I mean just just like  anything else with real form it's it's  really easy to test your functions and  you can write you know a few few lines  to change some variables and account for  different things that the user might do  and then you can run a test and set that  up as a test and then when you run it  you'll easily see if you if you broken  anything with the past version stuff  like that which which I tend to do so  it's nice to have this bug reporting so  right away when I first built this I  built in the upper right corner this  little button report bug and it uses the  the send mail function to basically send  me send me a email and with that email  I'll get information about all the  variables and that utility what current  utility they're using and it also sent  me you can take a front-end screen shot  using Mathematica code and it'll send me  a screenshot of that and lots of times I  can see you know what are the red boxes  or something you can if something's not  working right but you really need some  some way to do that because Wolfram's  really good about not 