nt  configuration and I wanted something  that was really generic to do that and  so I basically wrote this this little  code right here and it'll take the name  whatever the utility is and then all my  variables associated with that it'll  write them as an association to a file  with the string as the variable and then  the value and then similarly when I load  that back in I could take those those  same strings and just convert them into  into symbols and then set the values and  it works really nicely and I don't ever  have to manually go through and set  which variables I want to save it just  it's handled for each utility okay  finally  so advantages of Wolfram language for  application development algorithms can  be developed very quickly and you  generally require a lot less code which  which I'm sure all of you are aware of  but when it comes to things like R&amp;D  projects it's really nice to have and  its really nice to do it that way  limitations for me as far as what I saw  the application development there were  some strong limitations and the UI  capabilities I don't think you're ever  going to be able to develop a  professional you know looking desktop  application you know there's just some  limits in the in the way the notebook is  set up but I mean I think you can get to  something that looks pretty nice and  especially if it's for quick you know  internal development I think it'll  probably meet any requirements that you  have  so conclusion so future Daleks we've got  a growing list of utilities dad right  now probably you know we're at 37 I  think I'll probably easily get up to 50  we're dealing a lot more with the lab  now with our lab and sort of coupling  the engineering work with the lab and  there's there's a lot of opportunities  there to expand further with that and  I've been really interested in the cloud  opportunities and learning specifically  about API functions his way to to call  those instead of running these things  through CDF